{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D7YHN6AR42oafWtCrdhqRd","type":"tool_result","content":"**Build and invocation**\n\n- Build with `lake build`.\n- Run the binary inside the Lake environment:\n\n```\nlake env <path to lean4export binary> <lean file without .lean suffix>+\n```\n\n- Example from the README, run from a `mathlib4` checkout:\n\n```\nlake env ../.lake/build/bin/lean4export Mathlib > out.ndjson\n```\n\nThis exports the named module and its transitive dependencies. Multiple module names can be passed, separated by spaces.\n\n**Selective export**\n\nYes. A list of specific declarations can be given after a `--` separator that follows the module names. The README doesn't name a separate option for this.\n\n**Options**\n\n- `--export-unsafe` includes unsafe declarations, which may help test or debug other tools.\n- `--export-mdata` includes `Expr.mdata` items, which are removed by default. (The README writes this as `--export-mdata\"`, with a stray quote.)\n\n**Format version**\n\nThe README doesn't state a version number. It links to `format_ndjson.md` and describes the output as the \"Lean 4 NDJSON export format.\"\n\n**Lean toolchain**\n\nThe README doesn't specify one. The repository includes a `lean-toolchain` file, but its contents aren't shown on the page."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e952065-9d21-48cd-9ba0-35babc908345","uuid":"dbf932e6-800b-4159-9465-b0978a590374","timestamp":"2026-11-05T01:17:21.590Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":234721,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Build and invocation**\n\n- Build with `lake build`.\n- Run the binary inside the Lake environment:\n\n```\nlake env <path to lean4export binary> <lean file without .lean suffix>+\n```\n\n- Example from the README, run from a `mathlib4` checkout:\n\n```\nlake env ../.lake/build/bin/lean4export Mathlib > out.ndjson\n```\n\nThis exports the named module and its transitive dependencies. Multiple module names can be passed, separated by spaces.\n\n**Selective export**\n\nYes. A list of specific declarations can be given after a `--` separator that follows the module names. The README doesn't name a separate option for this.\n\n**Options**\n\n- `--export-unsafe` includes unsafe declarations, which may help test or debug other tools.\n- `--export-mdata` includes `Expr.mdata` items, which are removed by default. (The README writes this as `--export-mdata\"`, with a stray quote.)\n\n**Format version**\n\nThe README doesn't state a version number. It links to `format_ndjson.md` and describes the output as the \"Lean 4 NDJSON export format.\"\n\n**Lean toolchain**\n\nThe README doesn't specify one. The repository includes a `lean-toolchain` file, but its contents aren't shown on the page.","durationMs":6080,"url":"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D7YHN6AR42oafWtCrdhqRd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019JoDPcnyKsKN41qv6GPfnu","type":"tool_result","content":"**Supported Lean versions and export formats**\n- The README describes nanoda_lib as an external type checker for Lean 4.\n- It does not name a specific Lean 4 version or pin one.\n- Export files come from [lean4export](https://github.com/leanprover/lean4export). The README notes that lean4export already uses JSON as its export format.\n- The README does not say whether it consumes lean4export output directly. It only says export files can be created with that tool.\n\n**Build and run**\n- Build and run with cargo: `cargo run --release -- <path_to_config>`, or build first with `cargo build --release` and then run the built binary.\n- The binary takes one argument: a path to a JSON configuration file. The README says there is no traditional CLI.\n- Export files can be streamed through stdin by setting `use_stdin: true` in the config.\n- The README says building with cargo should work on modern platforms without extra steps.\n- The library can be used as a normal Rust crate by listing it as a dependency in `Cargo.toml`.\n\n**Usage lines (verbatim)**\n> \"Either run the binary directly through cargo `cargo run --release -- <path_to_config>`\"\n\n> \"or build the binary using `cargo build --release`, then run the built binary\"\n\n**Last mention of a date**\n- The README contains no date. The only year on the page is the footer's \"© 2026 GitHub, Inc.\", which is not a README date."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e952065-9d21-48cd-9ba0-35babc908345","uuid":"d252f38d-3aa0-4209-99ab-4e10966255b8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T01:17:21.652Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":246196,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Supported Lean versions and export formats**\n- The README describes nanoda_lib as an external type checker for Lean 4.\n- It does not name a specific Lean 4 version or pin one.\n- Export files come from [lean4export](https://gi