{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019YFV7F3qWPcKREFxQXgerU","type":"tool_result","content":"59:Independent replay\nIndependent replay\n\nThe repository holds no recorded #print axioms output for the family, no continuous integration that builds its Lean, and enable_nanoda: false in the comparator’s JSON (Section 4).\nWe therefore built the solution’s import closure ourselves, in isolation, at the toolchain the repository pins, and recorded what the kernel reports. This section says exactly what was run, where,\nand what came out; the scripts are quoted verbatim in paper3/replay/README.md, and the\nlogs are under paper3/replay/logs/.\n\n5.1\n\nWhat was run, and where\n\nA fresh copy of the clone (commit 4eaee875) was built inside a bwrap (bubblewrap) sandbox on the project’s machine: the system directories bound read-only, a private tmpfs home\nwith nothing of the real home or of this repository visible, separate PID, IPC and UTS namespaces, the whole sandbox confined to eight cores (taskset -c 0-7) at the lowest scheduling\npriority, and the network on, because the repository’s lakefile clones its 41 dependencies\nat build time and the Mathlib object files come from the Mathlib cache. Inside, elan was\ninstalled into the private home and fetched the pinned toolchain from the repository’s own\nlean-toolchain file; then, in the clone’s lean/ directory, lake exe cache get, lake\nbuild OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.Main (the solution’s closure, not the collection’s default target, which builds all 721 families), and #print axioms of the one theorem the\ncomparator names, OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat,\nthrough lake env lean. Only text left the sandbox; nothing built inside it was executed outside it.\nTwo runs were needed. The first, started at 01:03 on 2026-11-05, failed at once at the build\nstep because its command line carried -j 8, an option this version of Lake does not have (the\nwhole of its build log is the one line error: unknown short option ’-j’); nothing of\nthe family was compiled. The second, started at 01:16 with that option removed and the core\ncap moved to taskset, is the run reported below. Both scripts, both versions, and the launch\ncommand are in the README; the first run’s logs are kept beside the second’s.\n\n2\n\n\f5.2\n\nWhat came out\n\nThe build finished with exit code 0 in 33 minutes of wall clock on eight cores (01:16:25 to 01:49:31),\ncompiling 3,091 modules with the prefix OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT—exactly the\nimport closure the inspection of Section 4 enumerated, the same 3,091 names with an empty set\ndifference both ways (paper3/replay/counts.sh; Lake’s own job total, 12014, counts the\ncached Mathlib and dependency modules, none of which was compiled). The log has no line\ncontaining error and none containing sorry; its eleven warning lines are Lake noting that\nthe lakefile’s compatibility patches leave eleven dependencies with “local changes”, and none of\nthe eleven is Mathlib. Seventeen source files of the closure themselves carry #print axioms\ncommands, so the build log records nineteen axiom lines as it goes, every one [propext,\nClassical.choice, Quot.sound]; among them SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 197–\n201, prints the axioms of compiled_exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_and_sat, exact_count_eth,\nsat_polynomial_space and original_sat_main. The separate #print axioms run after\nthe build gives, for the comparator’s theorem,\n’OAI.Algorithms.SubexponentialSAT.SqrtLogSpace.exact_count_and_sat’\ndepends on axioms: [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]\n\n(paper3/replay/logs/run2/axioms.txt). The versions are Lean (version 4.34.1, x86_64-unknow\ncommit 5045d005..., Release), Lake version 5.0.0-src+5045d00, and Mathlib at\nd13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612 from the clone’s own manifest, with its\nobject files from the Mathlib cache (8,908 files). The replay copy is at commit 4eaee875, the\nsame as the pristine clone, and the comparator file and the solution’s files hash identically in\nboth (paper3/replay/logs/run2/hashes.txt; the comparator statement’s SHA-256 begins\n4183b205). The full build log is kept out of the repository by size; its SHA-256 (d313f594...)\nand its last 200 lines are committed.\nSo the sentence every later section relies on reads: replayed—the solution’s closure builds from\nsource at the pinned toolchain with upstream Mathlib, and the kernel reports the three standard\naxioms and nothing else for the theorem the comparator names. This moves the first three items\nof the inspection’s trust boundary (Section 4): the build is no longer OpenAI’s unpublished\none, the #print axioms output is now on record, and the Mathlib revision was fetched from\nupstream and not patched.\n\n5.3\n\nThe second kernel\n\nThe comparator’s JSON for the family has enable_nanoda: false: OpenAI’s check of this\nfamily ran the Lean kernel only. We ran a second kernel, in the same sandbox, after the build\n(paper3/replay/nanoda.sh, recorded in the README before it ran): lean4export (tag\nv4.34.0, commit 076e8e57, built with the pinned v4.34.1 toolchain so that it reads the\nobject files the build produced) exported exact_count_and_sat and its transitive dependencies from the built environment—2,355,666,449 bytes of NDJSON, 43,633,742 lines, format version 3.1.0, Lean githash 5045d005..., in 68 seconds—and nanoda_lib (nanoda contributors,\n2026) (version 0.4.31, commit 8de7e0c4, 2026-10-29; an external type checker for Lean 4 written in Rust, which shares no code with the Lean kernel) checked that export with propext,\nClassical.choice and Quot.sound as the permitted axioms and the Nat and String literal extensions on. Its output (paper3/replay/logs/nanoda/nanoda\\_check.txt):\n3\n\n\fChecked 95176 declarations with no errors\nin 110 seconds on eight cores, exit code 0, after printing back the theorem’s statement and the\nthree axioms from its own parse of the ex